## Where each record goes

Each kind of record has one place. Before you write one, find its row; a record
written somewhere else is a record the next session cannot find.

| What a reader asks | Where it is |
| --- | --- |
| What changed, and when | the commit log and the merge request |
| Why this way, what was verified | the merge request description, which becomes the squash commit body |
| How a ticket ended, what was not verified | that issue's `## Outcome` |
| What was decided in a ticket | that issue's `## Decisions` |
| How a question was researched and decided | a record issue, one question each, ending in `## Decision` |
| A design decision to keep | an ADR, when [domain.md](domain.md)'s three conditions hold |
| The rule itself | the spec or the standard the project keeps |
| What is still to do, and a rule across tickets | the open issue a closed ticket's `## Outcome` links, or a map note for a ticket not cut yet; a rule across tickets in the map |

A document states the rule; whether it is done is the issue's to say.

A merge is not noted anywhere else: the merge request, the issue's `## Outcome` and
`git log` record it.

The map holds only what is still in force: a rule that holds across tickets, a
note for a ticket not cut yet, or a change to the map or to a milestone's
status. A note leaves the map when its ticket is cut.

Point at another record by its issue and section or rule name, never at a dated
line of the map: the map's lines move or leave.

## Wayfinding operations

A **map** is one issue; tickets are its **child** issues. The `/wayfinder`
skill can work a map with these steps, but plinth does not install it: it is
listed only, and `claude plugin install mattpocock-skills-extra@plinth` adds
it. Without it, follow the steps by hand.
GitLab Free has no blocking links between issues, so blocking is written in
the body.

- **Map**: one issue labelled `wayfinder:map`. `glab issue create --label wayfinder:map`
- **Child ticket**: add it to the map's task list and start the child body with `Part of #<map>`. Label: `wayfinder:<type>` (`research` / `prototype` / `grilling` / `task`). Assign yourself when you claim it.
- **Create a child**: a `research`, `prototype` or `grilling` child (and the map itself) is a record, so start its body from its kind's skeleton under [Record skeletons](#record-skeletons), with its title prefix and every section; a `task` child starts from the Task template. The wayfinder skill's own ticket body, `## Question` alone with the answer as a resolution comment, gives way to it: the answer goes into the child's `## Decision`. The skill's rule that a map and its tickets carry only `wayfinder:` labels gives way to this page's triage labels. A skill's example format does not override this page.
- **Already written another way**: edit each record in place, fetching its body fresh first (`glab issue view <n> -F json --jq .description`): add the missing sections and, where its language departs from `AGENTS.md`'s convention, rewrite it in the project's language, keeping every fact. Do not close and re-create it: that breaks links, sub-issue links and the map's task list. The edit history keeps the old text.
- **Blocking**: start the child body with `Blocked by: #<n>, #<n>`. Unblocked once every blocker is closed.
- **Frontier query**: open children of the map with no open blocker and no assignee, first in map order.
- **Claim**: `glab issue update <n> --assignee +<your username>` (`glab auth status` names it), the session's first write.
- **Resolve**: put the answer in the body — the shape the table under Four kinds of issue gives that child's kind, and the body is the record, a comment is not. A `research`, `prototype` or `grilling` child is a record: `## Findings` and `## Decision`, and `## Decision` is its ending, so it does not also take an `## Outcome`. A `task` child is a task like any other and ends in `## Outcome`. Then `glab issue close <n>`. The map gains one line (gist + link) under its Decisions-so-far only when the answer is a rule that holds across tickets; a closed ticket is not noted there.

## Record skeletons

A record starts from its kind's skeleton: write the block to a file, fill in
each `<…>` or leave it for the work to fill, and pass the file with `--description-file`.
Every section stays, empty or not; the title keeps its English prefix. The
description is written in the language `AGENTS.md` names for records.

### A map (`wayfinder:map`)

`glab issue create --title "Map: <destination>" --label wayfinder:map --label needs-triage --description-file <file>`

```markdown
## Destination

<what reaching the end of this map looks like, in one or two lines>

## Notes

<the domain, the skills every session reads, standing preferences>

## Decisions so far

<one line (gist + link) per rule that holds across tickets>

## Not yet specified

<questions coming that cannot be stated sharply yet>

## Out of scope

<work ruled out of this map, with the closed issue that ruled it out>
```

### Research (`wayfinder:research`)

`glab issue create --title "Research: <question>" --label wayfinder:research --label needs-triage --description-file <file>`

```markdown
Part of #<map>

## Question

<the one question this record answers>

## Findings

<what was read, tried or asked, with links; empty until the work starts>

## Decision

<the answer and its reason; this section is the record's ending>

## Used by

<the issues and merge requests that act on the decision; empty until one does>
```

### A grilling (`wayfinder:grilling`)

`glab issue create --title "Decision: <question>" --label wayfinder:grilling --label needs-triage --description-file <file>`

```markdown
Part of #<map>

## Question

<the one question this record answers>

## Findings

<what was read, tried or asked, with links; empty until the work starts>

## Decision

<the answer and its reason; this section is the record's ending>

## Used by

<the issues and merge requests that act on the decision; empty until one does>
```

### A prototype (`wayfinder:prototype`)

`glab issue create --title "Prototype: <question>" --label wayfinder:prototype --label needs-triage --description-file <file>`

```markdown
Part of #<map>

## Question

<the one question this record answers>

## Findings

<what was read, tried or asked, with links; empty until the work starts>

## Decision

<the answer and its reason; this section is the record's ending>

## Used by

<the issues and merge requests that act on the decision; empty until one does>
```
